            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus identifies elders primarily as authoritative leaders of God's people. The twenty-four elders of Revelation are most commonly interpreted as representing the totality of redeemed humanity (such as the 12 tribes and 12 apostles), with alternatives including angelic beings in a divine council or symbols of the 24 priestly courses.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book identifies elders as leaders and representatives of assemblies in a heavenly court, explicitly equating them with the constellations and stars among the host of heaven.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Redeemed human representatives (e.g., glorified church/Israel) or angelic beings.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Leaders and representatives of assemblies in a heavenly court, specifically identified as constellations and stars.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus limits the symbolic identity of the elders to human representatives or non-astral angelic beings, whereas the book introduces a cosmological identification, identifying them as constellations and stars.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus predominantly views the elders as representing human groups (like the 12 tribes and 12 apostles) or a literal angelic council, whereas the book asserts they are astral bodies (constellations) functioning as the heavenly representatives of assemblies.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"Although the book and consensus agree on the functional role of the elders ('representatives in a court around the throne'), the book's core ontological identification of these figures as constellations introduces an astral referent that fundamentally diverges from the consensus's anthropological or purely angelological identifications.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"PARTIAL\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"While biblical texts frequently associate the heavenly host with stars (and Revelation 1 explicitly links stars to angels of the churches), the text of Revelation 4 does not explicitly identify the twenty-four elders as stars or constellations, leaving the astral connection implicit or interpretive rather than strictly textual.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
